<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">cooking</ansi>

Raw game meat needs a hearth before it's worth eating. The <ansi fg="skill">Cooking</ansi> skill
turns meat and gathered herbs into proper meals at a hearth, a fixture
found in some rooms. You can also cook at a lit campfire (see below).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How to cook</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">use hearth</ansi>
  Cooks a meal. The hearth checks its recipes in a fixed order, skips
  any whose ingredients aren't all there or whose Cooking level you
  haven't reached, and makes the first one left. It consumes the
  ingredients and adds the finished meal to the hearth for you to take.

  <ansi fg="command">look hearth</ansi>
  Lists every recipe the hearth knows, and names the Cooking level any
  of them requires.

A recipe with no skill requirement can be cooked by anyone. One that
needs a level you don't have is simply skipped; if every ready recipe
is out of your reach, cooking there tells you the lowest level you'd
need and makes nothing, without using up any ingredients.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where to cook</ansi>

The Waymark Inn's hearth (Dunmar) makes its meals from raw game meat, wild
thyme and fish, gathered on the road:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Seared game meat</ansi>    1 meat                    Cooking 1
  <ansi fg="yellow">Thyme-roasted game</ansi>  1 meat, 1 wild thyme      Cooking 2
  <ansi fg="yellow">Hunter's stew</ansi>       2 meat, 1 wild thyme      Cooking 3

Fish gathered with <ansi fg="command">fish</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help gathering</ansi>) cooks too:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Grilled fish</ansi>       1 raw fish                Cooking 1

The Waymark Inn also trains Cooking up to level 4 (<ansi fg="command">help train</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Meal buffs</ansi>

Eating a cooked meal gives the member who eats it a buff for its next
few battles (battles, not hours: it never wears off while you travel).
One buff at a time; a new meal replaces the old one. Each battle the
member fights counts one off.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Meal</ansi>                <ansi fg="yellow">Buff</ansi>           <ansi fg="yellow">Battles</ansi>  <ansi fg="yellow">Effect</ansi>
  Seared game meat    Strong         2        +5% damage dealt
  Thyme-roasted game  Steady         3        +5% damage, 5% less damage taken
  Hunter's stew       Hearty         3        10% less damage taken
  Grilled fish        Clear-headed   3        20% of max mana back as each battle begins

<ansi fg="command">eat</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">company eat</ansi> name the buff; <ansi fg="command">survival</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">status</ansi> show the battles
left. A meal's buff adds to the member's battle condition (<ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Cooking at camp</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp cook</ansi>
  At your own camp with its fire lit, the best cook with you cooks the
  same meals, the first their Cooking allows, from company cargo.
  The meal goes into the same cargo. A ranger's forage
  fills the cargo with meat and thyme (<ansi fg="command">help forage</ansi>). You can't cook
  in a fight.

An iron cookpot in the company's gear (<ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>) stretches a dish made
from more than one ingredient into one extra portion.

The best cook is whoever has the highest Cooking among you and the living
companions at the camp; you win a tie, then the companion with the lowest
number. The message names them ("Brannoc cooks hunter's stew over the
campfire; it goes into the company's cargo."), and
if nobody can make a dish, it says the best Cooking you have there.
Companions learn Cooking with <ansi fg="command">company train</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help company train</ansi>),
and some recruits arrive knowing it. An inn hearth (<ansi fg="command">use hearth</ansi>) uses
your own Cooking only.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The Cook profession</ansi>

The <ansi fg="skill">Cook</ansi> profession pairs Cooking with Track, for hunting the ingredients
a hearth wants.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help use</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help look</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help eat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help train</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help skills</ansi>.

Character > Skills lists Camp Cooking as a manual camp capability for
every archetype. It shows configured recipe ranks and reasons such as
missing ingredients, a higher rank, no lit camp here, battle or capacity.
It shows the best cook with you and their rank, not autoskill or a
companion specialist.
The view consumes nothing; only camp cook prepares the meal.
